WEBVTT

00:00.350 --> 00:01.210
- [Instructor] In this video,

00:01.210 --> 00:03.940
we're going to talk about
extraneous solutions.

00:03.940 --> 00:06.710
If you've never heard the term before,

00:06.710 --> 00:08.750
I encourage you to review some videos

00:08.750 --> 00:11.130
on Khan Academy on extraneous solutions.

00:11.130 --> 00:13.320
But just as a bit of a refresher,
it's the idea that you do

00:13.320 --> 00:15.960
a bunch of legitimate
algebraic operations,

00:15.960 --> 00:18.670
you get a solution or
some solutions at the end,

00:18.670 --> 00:21.020
but then when you test it
in the original equation,

00:21.020 --> 00:24.020
it doesn't satisfy the original equation.

00:24.020 --> 00:25.760
And so the key of this video

00:25.760 --> 00:30.560
is why do extraneous solutions even occur?

00:30.560 --> 00:35.160
And it all is due to the
notion of reversibility.

00:35.160 --> 00:39.010
There's certain operations in algebra

00:39.010 --> 00:40.910
that you can do in one
direction, but you can't,

00:40.910 --> 00:42.460
and it'll always be true in one direction,

00:42.460 --> 00:46.990
but it isn't always true
in the other direction.

00:46.990 --> 00:49.080
And I'll show you those two operations.

00:49.080 --> 00:52.370
One is squaring and the other
is multiplying both sides

00:52.370 --> 00:54.310
by a variable expression.

00:54.310 --> 00:57.050
So let's just see the example of squaring,

00:57.050 --> 00:59.600
and then we're going to see
it in an actual scenario

00:59.600 --> 01:02.890
where you're dealing with
an extraneous solution.

01:02.890 --> 01:06.700
So we know, for example,
that if a is equal to b,

01:06.700 --> 01:09.980
I could square both sides

01:09.980 --> 01:13.790
and then a squared is going
to be equal to b squared.

01:13.790 --> 01:16.320
But the other way is not true.

01:16.320 --> 01:21.190
For example, if a squared
is equal to b squared,

01:21.190 --> 01:26.190
it is not always the case
that a is equal to b.

01:26.360 --> 01:28.050
What's a example that shows

01:28.050 --> 01:29.340
that this is not always the case?

01:29.340 --> 01:31.840
Actually, pause the video,
try to think about it.

01:31.840 --> 01:34.100
Well, negative two squared

01:34.100 --> 01:36.480
is indeed equal to two squared,

01:36.480 --> 01:40.070
but negative two is not equal to two.

01:40.070 --> 01:44.080
So this shows that you can
square both sides of an equation

01:45.090 --> 01:48.860
and deduce something that is true,

01:48.860 --> 01:50.130
but the other way around

01:50.130 --> 01:53.120
is not necessarily going to be true.

01:53.120 --> 01:56.660
Another non-reversible operation sometimes

01:56.660 --> 02:00.400
is multiplying both sides
by a variable expression.

02:00.400 --> 02:04.440
So multiplying both sides,

02:04.440 --> 02:06.260
actually, like I said,
that will make us confused,

02:06.260 --> 02:07.480
looks like an x.

02:07.480 --> 02:12.480
Multiply both sides

02:12.810 --> 02:14.390
by variable.

02:14.390 --> 02:15.270
I'll just write variable,

02:15.270 --> 02:18.900
but it could be a variable
expression as well.

02:18.900 --> 02:23.900
For example, we know
that if a is equal to b,

02:23.940 --> 02:25.800
that if we multiply both
sides by a variable,

02:25.800 --> 02:26.870
that's still going to be true.

02:26.870 --> 02:30.143
xa is going to be equal to xb.

02:32.150 --> 02:34.930
But the other, the reverse,
isn't always the case.

02:34.930 --> 02:37.890
If xa is equal to xb,

02:37.890 --> 02:41.940
is it always the case
that a is equal to b?

02:41.940 --> 02:43.230
Well, the simple answer is no,

02:43.230 --> 02:45.000
and I always encourage
you, pause this video

02:45.000 --> 02:46.790
and see if you can find an example

02:46.790 --> 02:48.380
where this doesn't work.

02:48.380 --> 02:52.330
Well, if a was two and b is three

02:52.330 --> 02:55.660
and the variable x just happened
to take on the value zero.

02:55.660 --> 02:57.200
So we know that zero times two

02:57.200 --> 02:59.820
is indeed equal to zero times three,

02:59.820 --> 03:04.380
but two is not equal to three.

03:04.380 --> 03:05.920
Now, how does all this connect

03:05.920 --> 03:08.740
to the extraneous solutions you've seen

03:08.740 --> 03:10.710
when you were solving radical equations

03:10.710 --> 03:12.510
or when you were solving some rational,

03:12.510 --> 03:15.880
or equations with rational
expressions on both sides?

03:15.880 --> 03:17.090
Well, let's look at an example.

03:17.090 --> 03:19.010
Let's solve a radical equation.

03:19.010 --> 03:22.530
If I wanted to solve the equation

03:22.530 --> 03:27.510
the square root of five x minus four

03:27.510 --> 03:29.990
is equal to x minus two,

03:29.990 --> 03:32.380
a typical first step is, hey,
let's get rid of this radical

03:32.380 --> 03:35.520
by squaring both sides.

03:35.520 --> 03:39.160
So I'm going to square both sides,

03:39.160 --> 03:41.730
and then I'm going to
get five x minus four

03:41.730 --> 03:45.030
is equal to x squared
minus four x plus four.

03:45.030 --> 03:47.060
Once again, if this looks
completely unfamiliar to you,

03:47.060 --> 03:48.810
we go into much more depth in other videos

03:48.810 --> 03:51.610
where we introduce the
idea of radical equations.

03:51.610 --> 03:55.860
And let's see, we can subtract
five x from both sides.

03:55.860 --> 03:57.450
We can add four to both sides.

03:57.450 --> 04:00.480
I'm just trying to get a
zero on the left hand side.

04:00.480 --> 04:01.740
And so I'm going to be left with

04:01.740 --> 04:06.210
zero is equal to x squared
minus nine x plus eight,

04:06.210 --> 04:11.210
or zero is equal to x minus
eight times x minus one,

04:11.640 --> 04:14.920
or we could say that x
minus one is equal to zero,

04:14.920 --> 04:18.230
or x minus eight is equal to zero.

04:18.230 --> 04:22.110
We get x equals one or x equals eight.

04:22.110 --> 04:23.520
So let's test these solutions.

04:23.520 --> 04:26.240
If x equals eight, we would get,

04:26.240 --> 04:28.000
and I'll color code it a little bit,

04:28.000 --> 04:30.770
for x equals eight, if I test
it in the original equation,

04:30.770 --> 04:35.410
I get the square root
of 36 is equal to six,

04:35.410 --> 04:38.370
which absolutely true, so that one works.

04:38.370 --> 04:40.210
But what about x equals one?

04:40.210 --> 04:45.020
I get the square root of five
times one minus four is one

04:45.020 --> 04:49.030
is equal to one minus two,
which is equal to negative one.

04:49.030 --> 04:50.760
That did not work.

04:50.760 --> 04:55.180
This right over here is
an extraneous solution.

04:55.180 --> 04:56.760
If someone said, what are all the x values

04:56.760 --> 04:59.330
that satisfy this equation,
you would not say x equals one,

04:59.330 --> 05:01.860
even though you got there with
legitimate algebraic steps.

05:01.860 --> 05:03.930
And the reason that is true is,

05:03.930 --> 05:06.220
actually, pause this video, look back.

05:06.220 --> 05:09.920
For which of these steps
does x equal one still work

05:09.920 --> 05:12.150
and what step does it not work?

05:12.150 --> 05:14.960
Well, you'll see that x equals one

05:14.960 --> 05:19.070
works for all of these equations
below this purple line.

05:19.070 --> 05:20.900
It just doesn't work for

05:20.900 --> 05:23.180
the square root of five minus four x

05:23.180 --> 05:25.410
is equal to x minus two.

05:25.410 --> 05:27.670
In fact, you could start with x minus one

05:27.670 --> 05:31.520
and then you could deduce all
the way up to this line here.

05:31.520 --> 05:32.890
But the issue here is that

05:32.890 --> 05:36.040
squaring is not a reversible operation.

05:36.040 --> 05:38.030
This is analogous to saying, hey,

05:38.030 --> 05:40.070
we know that a squared
is equal to b squared.

05:40.070 --> 05:42.860
We know that this is equal to this.

05:42.860 --> 05:46.970
But then that doesn't mean that
a is necessarily equal to b

05:46.970 --> 05:49.220
for x equals one.

05:49.220 --> 05:52.760
And we could do the same
thing with a rational,

05:52.760 --> 05:55.900
or an equation that deals
with rational expressions.

05:55.900 --> 05:58.650
So, for example, we
might have to deal with,

05:58.650 --> 06:01.150
and let me make sure I
have some space here.

06:01.150 --> 06:05.870
If I had to solve x
squared over x minus one

06:05.870 --> 06:08.770
is equal to one over x minus one,

06:08.770 --> 06:10.270
the first thing I might wanna do

06:10.270 --> 06:13.720
is multiply both sides by x minus one.

06:13.720 --> 06:18.700
So multiply by x minus one.

06:18.700 --> 06:20.340
Now, notice I'm multiplying both sides

06:20.340 --> 06:21.940
by a variable expression, so we have to be

06:21.940 --> 06:23.500
a little bit contentious now,

06:23.500 --> 06:26.070
but if I'd multiply both
sides by x minus one,

06:26.070 --> 06:28.440
I'm going to get x
squared is equal to one,

06:28.440 --> 06:30.550
or I could say that x equals one

06:30.550 --> 06:32.560
or x is equal to negative one.

06:32.560 --> 06:34.800
Well, we could test these.

06:34.800 --> 06:37.210
For x equals one, if I go up here,

06:37.210 --> 06:39.340
I'm dividing by zero on both sides.

06:39.340 --> 06:41.480
So this is an extraneous solution.

06:41.480 --> 06:44.480
They key here is that
we multiplied both sides

06:44.480 --> 06:45.670
by a variable expression.

06:45.670 --> 06:49.330
In this case, we multiplied
both sides by x minus one.

06:49.330 --> 06:50.530
You can do that.

06:50.530 --> 06:53.390
You can multiply both sides
by a variable expression,

06:53.390 --> 06:55.610
and it is a legitimate
algebraic operation.

06:55.610 --> 07:00.530
It's completely analogous to
what we saw right over here.

07:00.530 --> 07:03.670
Just because zero times two
is equal to zero times three

07:03.670 --> 07:06.370
does not mean that two is equal to three.

07:06.370 --> 07:07.620
It's completely analogous

07:07.620 --> 07:09.540
because we multiplied
by a variable expression

07:09.540 --> 07:12.370
that actually takes on the value zero

07:12.370 --> 07:14.790
when x is equal to one.

07:14.790 --> 07:18.420
So the big takeaway here
is, hopefully you understand

07:18.420 --> 07:21.870
why extraneous solutions
happen a little bit more.

07:21.870 --> 07:24.360
When you square, when
you multiply both sides

07:24.360 --> 07:26.760
by a variable expression,
completely legitimate

07:26.760 --> 07:28.500
as long as you do it properly,

07:28.500 --> 07:30.410
but it's not always the case

07:30.410 --> 07:32.980
that the reverse is true.

07:32.980 --> 07:34.970
You could add or subtract anything

07:34.970 --> 07:36.060
from both sides of an equation,

07:36.060 --> 07:38.100
and that's always going to be reversible.

07:38.100 --> 07:41.020
And so that's not going to
lead to extraneous solutions.

07:41.020 --> 07:42.390
You could multiply or divide

07:42.390 --> 07:45.360
by a non-zero constant value.

07:45.360 --> 07:48.200
That's also not going to
lead to anything shady,

07:48.200 --> 07:49.750
but if you're squaring both sides

07:49.750 --> 07:52.200
or multiplying both sides
by a variable expression,

07:52.200 --> 07:53.950
you should be a little bit careful.
